---
/**
 * Fender Telecaster dating guide. SECOND OF THE MODEL-AXIS SPOKES.
 *
 * Sibling of fender-stratocaster-dating-guide.astro. Same job, different model:
 * one guitar read year by year, where the method spokes read one method across
 * every model. Built on the branch joe/tele-dating-spoke, stacked on top of
 * joe/strat-dating-spoke because both pages edit the hub's "Go Deeper" chips
 * and the sitemap list and would otherwise conflict.
 *
 * WHAT MAKES THE TELE PAGE DIFFERENT FROM THE STRAT PAGE. Do not copy the Strat
 * structure over wholesale; the model's story is genuinely different:
 *   - The headline value line is blackguard vs whiteguard, not slab vs veneer.
 *   - The model changed NAME four times in two years (Esquire, Broadcaster,
 *     Nocaster, Telecaster), which no other Fender did.
 *   - The serial sat on the BRIDGE PLATE first, which is a Tele/Esquire thing.
 *   - Top loader vs string-through is a Tele-only bridge question, and 1959
 *     transition plates carry BOTH sets of holes.
 *   - The saddles change four times, more than any other single Tele feature.
 *   - The headstock never grew, and the spaghetti logo ran about a year longer
 *     than it did on the Strat.
 *   - The neck-stamp model code is 3 and never changed at the end-of-1965
 *     renumber, so unlike the Strat's 2/13/22 it does NOT help narrow the year.
 *
 * JOE'S REVIEW RULES FROM THE STRAT PAGE, applied here from the start:
 *   - Features give a WINDOW, often a wider one than the serial. They
 *     corroborate the serial rather than beating it. Only the neck heel date
 *     and the body date give a month. Nothing here may imply otherwise.
 *   - The serial belongs in the second pass, read as one vote among several.
 *   - Never call a feature "model-specific" without checking. Say what the
 *     Telecaster did and let the reader read it against their own guitar.
 *   - Never say "bare wood" about a body date. Say the date is written
 *     directly on the body. ("Bare wood" is the NAIL HOLE tell and means
 *     something specific and different.)
 *   - Keep a photo caption on the one thing the photo is for.
 *
 * LOGO COLOR. On a STANDARD Telecaster the spaghetti logo is SILVER. This is
 * NOT the same as the Stratocaster, where Joe confirmed it is gold. The 1959
 * Telecaster guide states the split directly: "silver on the standard model and
 * gold on the Custom." Do not "correct" silver to gold on this page.
 *
 * Content facts trace to Joe's own live pages and VG_FACTS.md: VG-0053 (dating
 * methodology), VG-0056 / VG-0057 (pickup dating and era), VG-0066 (1950s
 * serials, bridge plate to neck plate), VG-0067 (L-series), VG-0068 (big-F),
 * VG-0069 (blackguard span, summer-1954 white guard, and the Esquire to
 * Broadcaster to Nocaster to Telecaster name sequence), VG-0072 (pre-CBS
 * definition), VG-0073 / VG-0074 (custom color and refinish value).
 *
 * On-site sources: fender-physical-features.astro (guard, saddle, tuner, logo
 * and patent-block tables), fender-pot-codes.astro (supplier eras, the
 * capacitor table including the Esquire and dark-circuit rows, 1 Meg on the
 * Tele from around 1969), fender-pickup-dates.astro (flat to staggered poles
 * in late 1955, Formvar to plain enamel in 1964), fender-neck-dates.astro
 * (model code 3 for Telecaster and Esquire), and the 1952 and 1959 Telecaster
 * authentication guides plus the Paisley guide.
 *
 * NOT YET IN VG_FACTS, flagged for a joes-vintage-databases PR: the top-loader
 * window and the dual-hole transition plate; the mid-1959 no-date neck-heel
 * window; the Telecaster patent-block sequence (none, then three from late
 * 1961, then two from 1965); the four-stage Telecaster saddle run; and the
 * original three-position wiring scheme.
 *
 * NO YEAR-TO-SERIAL-NUMBER TABLE, same as the Strat page. VG-0066 / VG-0067 /
 * VG-0068 are all flagged KEEP FLAGGED in VG_FACTS on their numeric ranges.
 * The charts live once, on the hub.
 */
