const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What Year Is My Telecaster?",
    a: "Work it in three passes and expect each one to give you a window rather than an answer. Start with the pickguard color and the bridge, because those two put the guitar in a broad era from across the room. Then read the saddles, the board, the knobs, and the serial number together, treating the serial as one vote among several. Then open it up for the parts Fender actually dated: the neck heel stamp and the body date, backed up by the pot codes. Those last two are the only things that will give you a month.",
  },
  {
    q: "What Is A Blackguard Telecaster?",
    a: "A Telecaster or Esquire built from 1950 to about the summer of 1954, when the pickguard was a single sheet of black phenolic. Fender moved to a single-ply white guard mid-1954, and everything from then to 1959 is a whiteguard. Those are not nicknames for a color, they are the two halves of the early Telecaster, and the changeover is the sharpest value line on the model. Leftover black guards were fitted after the change, so a 1954 can genuinely be either, and the guard alone does not settle the year.",
  },
  {
    q: "My Telecaster Strings Anchor At The Bridge Instead Of Through The Body. Is It Wrong?",
    a: "Probably not. Fender ran a top-loading bridge from about mid-1958 to mid-1959, where the strings anchor at the back of the bridge plate rather than passing through the body. It is correct and original for that window. Better still, plenty of 1959 bridge plates carry both sets of holes because Fender was mid-changeover, and a dual-hole plate is a genuine transition part rather than a modified one.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does A Spaghetti Logo Mean My Telecaster Is Pre-CBS?",
    a: "Not on its own, and this is where reading Stratocaster advice onto a Telecaster costs people money. The Strat moved to the transition logo in mid-1964, but the Tele kept the spaghetti logo into 1965. So a spaghetti logo on a 1965 Telecaster is correct and original, and dating one from the Strat's timeline will put it a year or more too early.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I Tell A Telecaster's Year From The Headstock Shape?",
    a: "No, and that is worth knowing because it is the one big clue a Stratocaster gives you that a Telecaster does not. Fender widened the Stratocaster headstock in late 1965, and the Mustang took the enlarged CBS headstock over the same stretch, but the Telecaster outline never changed. On a Tele you have to work from the logo, the patent numbers, and everything below the nut instead.",
  },
