---

<Layout
  title="How To Date A Fender Telecaster: Year By Year Guide"
  description="Date a vintage Fender Telecaster from what you can see: the guard, the bridge, the saddles, the board, and the logo, year by year from 1950 to 1971. From a vintage Fender appraiser."
  canonical={PAGE_PATH}
  ogImage={`${SITE_URL}${assetSrc(OG)}`}
  {...ogCardSize(OG)}
  ogImageAlt="A 1952 Fender Telecaster in butterscotch blonde with its original black phenolic pickguard"
  ogType="article"
  noIndex={false}
  structuredData={[articleSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <!-- Archetype B header backdrop: solid bands the absolute site Header sits on -->
  <div class="fspot-header-backdrop" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__dark"></div>
    <div class="fspot-header-backdrop__cream"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="fspot">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="hero" id="top">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <a class="back" href={HUB_HREF}>Back To The Full Fender Serial Guide</a>
        <span class="eyebrow">Fender Dating By Model</span>
        <h1>How To Date A Fender Telecaster</h1>
        <p class="lead">The Telecaster is harder to date than a Stratocaster for one reason: the headstock never changed shape, so the clue you can read from across a room does not exist. What you get instead is a guitar that changed its pickguard, its bridge, its saddles, its board, and even its name, each on a different schedule. Here is what changed and when, from the 1950 Esquire through 1971.</p>
        <div class="trust">
          <span>Joe Dampt</span><span>12 years buying vintage Fenders full-time</span><span>10,000+ appraised</span>
        </div>
        <figure class="sfig herofig">
          <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "1952-fender-telecaster-body-scaled.jpg")} alt="A 1952 Fender Telecaster in butterscotch blonde with its original single-ply black phenolic pickguard and brass saddles" width={1600} loading="eager" />
          <figcaption>A 1952 Telecaster. Black guard, brass saddles, serial in the bridge plate, and a one-piece maple neck. Four features, and between them they place this guitar before the summer of 1954 without anybody picking up a screwdriver.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- START HERE -->
    <section id="start">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Start Here</span>
        <h2>Read The Guitar In Three Passes</h2>
        <p>Nothing in the first two passes will hand you a single year, and it helps to know that going in. Brass saddles only say before the summer of 1954. A veneer board only says after August 1962. Each feature is a window, and several are wider than the serial number is. Their value is in agreeing with each other and with the number on the plate, because a guitar where six independent things all point at the same stretch of months is a guitar you can be confident about.</p>
        <p><b>First pass, across the room.</b> The pickguard and the bridge. Black or white, and strings through the body or anchored at the plate. Those two questions cut the vintage era into large pieces before you have touched the guitar.</p>
        <p><b>Second pass, in your hands.</b> The saddles, the board, the knobs, the logo, and the serial number. Take the serial as one vote among several rather than the answer, and read the plate style along with the digits, because where the number sits tells you more than the number does.</p>
        <p><b>Third pass, inside.</b> The neck heel stamp, the body date, and the pot codes. These are the only places Fender wrote an actual date, and the only things on the guitar that will give you a month rather than a window. This is also the only pass that needs tools, which is why it comes last.</p>
        <p><b>The three passes should agree.</b> When they do, you have a build date you can stand behind. When they don't, the disagreement is the interesting part, and it usually means a replaced neck, a swapped plate, or a guitar being sold as something it isn't.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SAFETY -->
    <section class="pt0" aria-label="Safety warning">
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="warnbox" role="note">
          <span class="warnbox__head"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"></path><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path></svg>Read This First</span>
          <p>Everything in the first two passes can be read with the guitar assembled. Don't take a vintage instrument apart unless you have done it before, because vintage screws, plastics, and finishes strip and crack easily, and an original blackguard is close to irreplaceable. If the outside checks leave you stuck, contact me before you reach for a screwdriver.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- FIVE FASTEST -->
    <section id="fast">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Short Version</span>
        <h2>The Five Fastest Checks</h2>
        <p>If you read nothing else on this page, read these five. Read them together rather than one at a time: no single row narrows anything to a year, but five rows overlapping do.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>What to look at</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>How to read it</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{fastRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="What to look at"><b>{r[0]}</b></td><td data-label="What it tells you" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="How to read it">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The Telecaster is the model people misdate by reading Stratocaster advice.</b> Two traps in particular. The Strat took the transition logo in mid-1964 and the Tele kept the spaghetti logo into 1965, so a spaghetti logo on a 1965 Tele is right and dating it from the Strat timeline puts it a year or more too early. And the Strat headstock grew in late 1965 while the Telecaster's never did, so the single easiest check on a Strat simply is not available here. Neither mistake is obvious while you are making it, and both turn into a bad offer in one direction or the other.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- YEAR BY YEAR -->
    <section id="years">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Whole Timeline</span>
        <h2>Telecaster Year By Year, 1950 To 1971</h2>
        <p>What changed each year, and the quickest way to see it. Two things to keep in mind while you read down the table. Fender never made a clean cutover, so a change dated to mid-year means both versions turn up on guitars from that year and a transition example is genuinely correct either way. And parts went into a bin before they went into a guitar, so a feature dates the part, and the guitar is that year or later.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What changed</th><th>Fastest tell</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{yearRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Year" class="yr">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="What changed">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="Fastest tell">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="note-sm">Past 1971 the guitar starts dating itself. From 1976 Fender moved the serial to a headstock decal with the year built into the prefix, and the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full serial guide</a> carries those charts along with the reissue and import prefixes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- GUARD -->
    <section id="guard">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">The Big One</span>
        <h2>Blackguard And Whiteguard</h2>
        <p>Collectors name whole eras of this guitar after its pickguard, which tells you how much the change matters. A <b>blackguard</b> is a Telecaster or Esquire from 1950 to about the summer of 1954, wearing a single sheet of thick black phenolic. Fender changed to a single-ply white guard mid-1954, and everything from then to 1959 is a <b>whiteguard</b>. Get the two the wrong way round and you have misdated the guitar by years and mispriced it by a great deal more.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "fender-telecaster-black-phenolic-pickguard-1024x683.jpg")} alt="The single-ply black phenolic pickguard on a 1952 Fender Telecaster, thick and hard with no layers visible at the edge" width={1024} />
            <figcaption>The black phenolic guard on a 1952. Thick, hard, and a single sheet, with no layers visible at the edge. Collectors call the material both Bakelite and phenolic.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "1959-fender-telecaster-blonde-1-scaled.jpg")} alt="A 1959 Fender Telecaster in blonde with its single-ply white pickguard, maple neck, and chrome ashtray bridge cover" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The whiteguard that replaced it, here on a 1959 in blonde. Still a single sheet, so the edge reads as one clean layer of white with no dark line running through it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={meta("fender-telecaster-white-pickguard-1966.jpg")} alt="A three-ply white plastic Fender Telecaster pickguard from 1966" width={1100} />
            <figcaption>The other end of the run, a three-ply white plastic guard on a 1966. Look at the edge and you can count the layers, which is the quickest way to tell it from the single-ply above. From mid-1965 the material is stable plastic, so it ages to cream at most and never to mint green.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Between the second and third of those came the three-ply cellulose nitrate guard, from 1959 to mid-1965. That one also left the factory white and turns mint green over decades as the nitrate breaks down, so on a Telecaster a genuinely mint guard points at that six-year window and nothing else. There is also a smell test that works: original nitrate has a faint sharp smell like vinegar, because that is the material slowly breaking down. Modern plastic has no smell at all.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">Count the layers at the edge before you count anything else. Single-ply and three-ply are the real dividing line, and a white guard on its own spans about twenty years.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>The guard is one of the most swapped parts on the guitar, so look at the wood instead.</b> On a blackguard the finish under the guard has been shielded from sixty years of light, so lifting it should reveal noticeably paler wood than the rest of the body. Collectors call that ghosting, and its absence on a claimed blackguard is a much bigger problem than the guard itself, because it points at a refinish.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "1952-fender-telecaster-ghosting-under-pickguard-1024x683.jpg")} alt="Pale unfaded wood revealed under the lifted pickguard of a 1952 Fender Telecaster, next to the darker butterscotch of the exposed body" width={1024} />
            <figcaption>Ghosting on a 1952. The wood the guard covered is a pale cream against the butterscotch of the rest of the body, which is what six decades of light does to an original finish.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <h3 id="names">Esquire, Broadcaster, Nocaster, Telecaster</h3>
        <p>No other Fender changed its name this often. The single-pickup <b>Esquire</b> arrives in 1950 and stays in the line for decades. The two-pickup version follows late in 1950 as the <b>Broadcaster</b>. Gretsch already had a Broadkaster drum kit and objected, so Fender simply cut the model name off the decal and kept shipping, leaving guitars wearing nothing but the Fender script. Those are the <b>Nocasters</b>, they exist only across 1951, and they are among the most valuable guitars the company ever built. Later in 1951 the name settles as <b>Telecaster</b>.</p>
        <p>Which means that on an early guitar the decal is a dating tool in itself. Read what is printed beside the Fender script, and if there is nothing there at all, look very carefully at everything else.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- BRIDGE -->
    <section id="bridge">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Turn It Over</span>
        <h2>The Bridge And The Saddles</h2>
        <p>The bridge carries two separate dating questions, and the first one is answered from the back of the guitar.</p>

        <h3 id="loader">Top Loader Or String-Through</h3>
        <p>A Telecaster normally strings through the body, with six ferrules visible on the back. From about the middle of 1958 to the middle of 1959 Fender ran a <b>top-loading</b> bridge instead, where the strings anchor at the back of the bridge plate and never pass through the body at all. It is correct and original for that window, and it is not a modification.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "1959-fender-telecaster-blonde-top-laoder-example-scaled.jpg")} alt="The top-loading bridge on a 1959 Fender Telecaster, with the strings anchoring at the back of the bridge plate" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>A top loader. The strings anchor at the rear edge of the bridge plate rather than passing down through the body.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "fender-telecaster-string-through-example-scaled.jpg")} alt="A string-through-body Fender Telecaster bridge, with the strings passing down through the body rather than anchoring at the plate" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>The standard arrangement either side of that window. The strings pass down through the body, so the back of the guitar carries six ferrules.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The genuinely useful part is the changeover. Plenty of 1959 bridge plates carry <b>both sets of holes</b>, because Fender was running through transition hardware, and a dual-hole plate is a real 1959 part rather than a compromise. If you have one, it is one of the tightest features on the whole guitar for placing the build inside a single year.</p>

        <h3 id="saddles">Four Saddles, Four Eras</h3>
        <p>The saddles changed more often than any other single part of the Telecaster, which makes them worth learning properly. There are three of them, each carrying two strings.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("brass-saddles-2.jpg")} alt="Brass Telecaster bridge saddles, 1950 to mid-1954, gold colored with a dull patina" width={1100} /><figcaption>Brass, 1950 to mid-1954. Gold brown, settling into a dull patina, and roughly 5/16 inch across.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("steelsaddles.jpg")} alt="Smooth flat-bottom steel Telecaster bridge saddles, mid-1954 to 1958" width={1100} /><figcaption>Smooth steel, mid-1954 to 1958. Bright silver, prone to rust, and roughly 1/4 inch across.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p>After those two come the <b>threaded barrels</b>, from 1958 to 1968, which are the ones most people picture on a vintage Telecaster, and then from 1968 a return to a <b>smooth barrel with a groove cut for each string</b>. So "steel saddles" on its own tells you almost nothing across a twenty-five year span, and knowing which of the three steels you are holding tells you a great deal.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Put calipers on brass saddles before you believe them.</b> The original brass ones run about 5/16 inch across and the steel that replaced them about 1/4 inch. Reproduction brass saddles are almost always made to the later 1/4 inch size, so brass saddles measuring 1/4 inch on a guitar sold to you as a 1952 are modern parts wearing the right color. This is the single most useful measurement on an early Telecaster.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK -->
    <section id="neck">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>The Neck And The Board</h2>
        <p>Four boards across the years. Through about April 1959 the neck is one piece of maple with the frets cut straight into it. Then a thick flat-bottomed slab of rosewood, about 4.8mm at the nut, glued on top. Partway through August 1962 the slab gives way to a thin curved veneer of roughly 2mm whose underside follows the radius of the neck. Then in the late 1960s maple returns, built the other way round as a separate cap glued over the truss rod.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "1959-fender-telecaster-blonde-maple-board-example.jpg")} alt="The one-piece maple neck of an early 1959 Fender Telecaster" width={1280} />
            <figcaption>An early 1959, still one-piece maple. Fender changed over around April, so both boards are correct for the year depending on the month.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "1959-fender-telecaster-slab-rosewood-board.jpg")} alt="The thick slab rosewood fingerboard of a late 1959 Fender Telecaster" width={1280} />
            <figcaption>A late 1959, with the thick slab rosewood board that replaced it. The glue line sits well down the neck at the headstock end.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p><b>The back of the neck settles the maple question.</b> A maple board on its own tells you almost nothing, because Fender used maple twice and the two look identical from the playing side. A one-piece 1950s neck has to carry a dark walnut skunk stripe down the back, since the truss rod went in from behind and the stripe is the lid on that channel, with a small walnut plug at the headstock end. The maple cap of the late 1960s has a separate board glued over the rod, so nothing needs filling from behind and the back is clean unbroken maple with no stripe and no plug.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1959-fender-telecaster-skunk-stripe.jpg")} alt="The back of a 1959 Fender Telecaster one-piece maple neck showing the dark walnut skunk stripe running down the center" width={1280} /><figcaption>A 1959 Telecaster, one-piece maple. The dark walnut stripe runs the length of the back, capping the truss rod channel.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1970-fender-telecaster-maple-cap.jpg")} alt="The back of a 1970 Fender Telecaster maple cap neck, clean maple with no skunk stripe" width={1100} /><figcaption>A 1970 Telecaster, maple cap. Same wood, same look from the front, and no stripe at all because the truss rod went in from the other side.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p>So a stripe on a claimed late-1960s maple-board Tele is wrong, and no stripe on a claimed 1950s one is equally wrong. The stripe returns for good in 1971 with the bullet truss rod, so from then on a maple Telecaster neck has one again.</p>
        <p>Two more neck details worth a look. The truss rod adjusts at the <b>heel</b> rather than the headstock throughout this era, and on the earliest guitars that adjustment screw is a <b>slot head</b>, not a Phillips. And the dots at the twelfth fret sat wide apart in the early 1950s and moved inward later, so their spacing is a rough era clue on its own.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- HEADSTOCK -->
    <section id="headstock">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two</span>
        <h2>Headstock, Logo And Tuners</h2>
        <p>Start with what the Telecaster does not give you. The outline never changed size. Fender widened the Stratocaster headstock in late 1965, and the Mustang took the enlarged CBS headstock across late 1965 into 1966, but the Telecaster kept its shape from 1950 onward, so the fastest check on a Strat is simply unavailable here. Everything on this headstock has to be read from the decal and the tuners instead.</p>

        <h3 id="logo">The Logo And The Patent Numbers</h3>
        <p>The spaghetti logo, thin and flowing, runs from 1950 <b>into 1965</b> on a Telecaster. That is about a year longer than on a Stratocaster, which moved to the transition logo in mid-1964, and it is the single most common way a Telecaster gets misdated. On a standard Telecaster the spaghetti decal is silver. The Telecaster Custom, introduced in 1959, wears a gold one instead, so the color separates the two models rather than two eras.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T59, "1959-fender-telecaster-blonde-headstock-scaled.jpg")} alt="The headstock of a 1959 Fender Telecaster with the silver spaghetti logo and two patent numbers below the model name" width={1600} />
            <figcaption>A 1959 headstock. The spaghetti decal sits on top of the finish with no clear coat over it, so you can catch its edge with a fingernail at a low angle.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p><b>The patent numbers are the better clue, because they changed more often.</b> For its first decade the Telecaster headstock carried <b>no patent numbers at all</b>, so a blank headstock under the model name points at 1950 through 1961. Late in 1961 a block of three appears: DES 164,227 for the body design, 2,573,254 for the bridge and pickup, and 2,784,631 for a lead and rhythm tone circuit that never became a standard feature. When the transition logo arrives in 1965 the block drops to two, 2,573,254 and 3,143,028, and the black CBS logo of 1967 keeps those two but shifts them below the Fender name.</p>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Read the block against the right model.</b> The Telecaster and the Stratocaster ran completely different patent numbers, and a reproduction decal that borrowed the wrong list is a common tell. One number that turns up in fakes and in careless online lists is 2,592,783, which is not a Fender patent at all. It belongs to a heat exchanger, so a headstock wearing it is wrong on its face. There is a full breakdown by model and era on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/">physical features guide</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 id="tuners">Tuners And The String Tree</h3>
        <p>Kluson made the tuners until the CBS years and the back of the housing tells you which era you are in: no text at all on the earliest, then KLUSON DELUXE in one vertical line from about mid-1956, then the same words split across two lines from about mid-1964. Fender's own F-stamped tuners became standard across the main lines in the middle to end of 1967.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "1952-fender-telecaster-kluson-no-line-tuners-1024x683.jpg")} alt="No-line Kluson tuners on a 1952 Fender Telecaster, with completely smooth housing backs" width={1024} /><figcaption>No line, on a 1952. The housing back is smooth, with nothing stamped on it at all.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("kluson-deluxe-single-line-tuner-1956-1964-on-vintage-fender-telecaster.jpg")} alt="A single-line Kluson Deluxe tuner on a vintage Fender Telecaster" width={1100} /><figcaption>Single line, from about mid-1956. KLUSON DELUXE runs down the housing in one column.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("kluson-deluxe-double-line-tuner-1964-1967-on-fender-telecaster.jpg")} alt="A double-line Kluson Deluxe tuner on a Fender Telecaster" width={1100} /><figcaption>Double line, from about mid-1964. The same words, split into two columns.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>Don't hang a year on a Kluson stamp.</b> The single-line to double-line changeover is genuinely disputed among researchers, with published dates ranging from the late 1950s to about 1964, and nobody has settled it. Double lines on a claimed 1962 are a reason to check the neck date and the pot codes, not a reason to walk away. What is not in dispute: PAT. APPLD. on the underside of a Kluson points to a pre-1958 build, because a full patent number replaced it around then.</p>
        </div>
        <p>The string tree is a smaller clue in the same place. Before about the middle of 1956 it is a small round metal guide; from then it is the wider butterfly, or wing, tree. It is an easy part to replace, so treat it as supporting evidence rather than proof.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "fender-telecaster-vintage-round-string-tree-1024x683.jpg")} alt="The small round string guide on the headstock of a 1952 Fender Telecaster" width={1024} /><figcaption>The round guide, before mid-1956.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("vintage-fender-butterfly-string-tree-1.jpg")} alt="A butterfly or wing string tree on a vintage Fender headstock" width={1100} /><figcaption>The butterfly tree, mid-1956 onward.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- NECK HEEL -->
    <section id="heel">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>The Neck Heel Date</h2>
        <p>The single most reliable dating reference on a pre-CBS Fender, and unlike everything above it, it is a date rather than a window. Fender wrote the month and year on the butt end of the neck heel, so the neck has to come off the body to read it. On a genuine old one the pencil or ink has aged into the grain.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={bmeta(T52, "1952-fender-telecaster-neck-heel-date-and-truss-rod-screw-1024x683.jpg")} alt="The neck heel of a 1952 Fender Telecaster showing the penciled date and the slot-head truss rod adjustment screw" width={1024} />
            <figcaption>A 1952 heel. The penciled date sits on the butt end, and the truss rod screw beside it is a slot head, which is correct for the year.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tip">
          <p><b>A blank heel is correct for the middle of 1959.</b> Fender told staff not to write dates on neck heels during a window that year, so a genuine mid-1959 Telecaster can have nothing there at all. That is a documented characteristic rather than a red flag, and it is worth knowing before you write off an honest guitar. Date one of those from the body date and the pot codes instead.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 id="code">The Model Code, And Why It Doesn't Help Here</h3>
        <p>The heel stamp usually opens with a model code, and on some Fenders that number is a strong dating clue on its own. Not on a Telecaster.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap compact"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Code on the heel</th><th>Model</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{codeRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Code" class="code">{r[0]}</td><td data-label="Model">{r[1]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The Telecaster is code 3 and stayed code 3. Fender renumbered the models at the end of 1965 and the Stratocaster moved from 2 to 13 and later to 22, which brackets a Strat neck before you read the date beside it. The Telecaster did not move, so the code confirms you are holding a Telecaster or Esquire neck and nothing more. That is still worth checking, because it is the fastest way to catch a neck that came off a different model, but do not expect it to narrow the year.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">The full decoder for the heel stamp, including the width letter, the pencil and ink eras, and the green stamp, is on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-neck-dates/">neck date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- BODY DATES -->
    <section id="body">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Three</span>
        <h2>Body Dates</h2>
        <p>The other place Fender wrote a real date, written directly on the body inside a cavity. Where it hides is model-dependent, and a Telecaster does not put it where a Stratocaster does, which is why people look in the wrong place and conclude their guitar has no date at all.</p>
        <p><b>On a Telecaster, check the bridge pickup route first.</b> Then the control cavity. The date can be penciled or written in ink, and on some guitars it is not on the wood at all but on a strip of masking tape stuck inside the cavity, sometimes with a worker's name alongside it.</p>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={meta("1956-fender-telecaster-blonde-21.jpg")} alt="A handwritten body date inside the bridge pickup route of a 1956 Fender Telecaster" width={1280} />
            <figcaption>A 1956 Telecaster with the date written in the bridge pickup route. This is the first place to look on a Tele.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="sfig">
            <LicensedImage src={meta("1956-fender-telecaster-blonde-24.jpg")} alt="A strip of masking tape inside the control cavity of a 1956 Fender Telecaster carrying a worker's name and a date" width={1280} />
            <figcaption>The same guitar's control cavity, where a strip of masking tape carries a worker's name and a date. Not every Fender body date is written on the body itself.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The body date is a corroborating date rather than the build date. It records when the body was routed and prepped, which can precede final assembly by weeks or several months, so read it as a floor. A body date and a neck date a few months apart is the normal, correct result on an untouched guitar.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">Where the date hides on every other Fender model, and what the ink stamps and tape markings look like, is on the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-body-dates/">body date guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- SERIAL -->
    <section id="serial">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Pass Two, One Vote</span>
        <h2>What The Serial Number Adds</h2>
        <p>A useful vote, and on an early Telecaster a more interesting one than on most Fenders, because of <b>where</b> it sits. The number will not give you a year on its own: plates were pre-stamped in batches, stored, and pulled off the stack in no order, and the published ranges overlap year to year and disagree between sources. Read it alongside the features rather than instead of them. What the serial tells you most reliably is which era it came from, and its location does that better than the digits do.</p>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table>
          <thead><tr><th>Where it sits</th><th>Years</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{plateRows.map((r) => (<tr><td data-label="Where it sits"><b>{r[0]}</b></td><td data-label="Years" class="yr">{r[1]}</td><td data-label="What it looks like">{r[2]}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="figrow gallery">
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("fender-bridge-plate-serial-number-scaled.jpg")} alt="A Fender Telecaster bridge plate with the serial number stamped into the metal, 1950 to 1954" width={1280} /><figcaption>The bridge plate serial, 1950 to about 1954. This is where the number lives on the earliest and most valuable Telecasters and Esquires.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1957-fender-esquire-blonde.jpg")} alt="The neck plate of a 1957 Fender Esquire with the serial number stamped at the top edge" width={1280} /><figcaption>A 1957 Esquire, after the number moved to the neck plate. The Esquire and the Telecaster share their serial scheme, their model code, and most of their timeline.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="sfig"><LicensedImage src={meta("1967-fender-f-plate-serial-number.jpg")} alt="A 1967 Fender big-F neck plate with a six-digit serial number stamped above a large stylized F" width={1280} /><figcaption>The big-F plate, from about the third quarter of 1965. The six digits sit above the large stylized F, not below it.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <p class="note-sm">Worth knowing why there is no Telecaster serial chart on this page: Fender serials are not model-specific. A 1963 neck plate reads exactly the same whether it ended up under a Telecaster, a Stratocaster, or a Jazz Bass, so the charts live once on the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a> rather than being copied onto every model page. The free lookup and every prefix from the 1950s through the modern reissues and imports are there too.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- LIMITS -->
    <section id="limits">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Keep It Honest</span>
        <h2>What The Features Can't Prove</h2>
        <p>Every feature on this page dates a part. None of them dates the guitar, and the difference matters most on exactly the instruments where the money is.</p>
        <p><b>A correct feature set is not proof of originality.</b> A Telecaster can wear a genuine 1952 neck, a genuine 1952 body, and genuine 1952 hardware and still be three guitars in a trenchcoat. Originality is a whole-instrument judgment that weighs the neck date, the body date, the pots, the pickups, the hardware, and the finish against each other and asks whether they tell one story.</p>
        <p><b>Transition dates are approximate, and honest guitars break them.</b> Fender phased changes in as parts bins emptied, so a guitar from a changeover year can legitimately carry either spec, or one of each. 1954 and 1959 are the two years where this happens most on a Telecaster, and both are years where a transitional example is correct rather than suspect.</p>
        <p><b>Features are the easiest thing to fake.</b> A reproduction decal, a set of relic'd tuners, brass-colored saddles cut to the modern size, and a correctly aged guard are all buyable, which is why the dated parts inside the guitar carry more weight than the parts you can see from outside. The finish is where the real money moves, and a refinished blackguard can lose a large share of its value against an original-finish example, so treat any finish call as a job for hands and UV rather than a photograph.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rule"></div>

    <!-- CROSS -->
    <section id="cross">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Put It All Together</span>
        <h2>Confirm It From The Inside</h2>
        <p>The <a class="spoke" href="#heel">neck heel date</a> and the <a class="spoke" href="#body">body date</a> above are the two that give you a month. Two more parts inside the guitar back them up. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pot-codes/">pot codes</a> date a potentiometer to the week, which is the tightest number anywhere on the guitar, though it is a floor rather than a build date because pots sat in bins. The <a class="spoke" href="/fender-pickup-dates/">pickup dates</a> corroborate, though on a Telecaster the neck pickup usually carries no date at all. For the parts view across every Fender model rather than this one, work through <a class="spoke" href="/fender-physical-features/">dating a Fender by its parts</a>, and if it turns out to be a Strat or an offset you are holding, the <a class="spoke" href="/fender-stratocaster-dating-guide/">Stratocaster</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/fender-jazzmaster-dating-guide/">Jazzmaster</a> dating guides are built the same way as this one.</p>
        <p>If you have already narrowed the year down, these go deeper on one specific Telecaster and show the real thing photographed up close: <a class="spoke" href="/post/1952-fender-telecaster-authentication-guide/">1952</a> and <a class="spoke" href="/post/1959-fender-telecaster-authentication-guide/">1959</a>, the two years that matter most on the model. There is also a guide to the <a class="spoke" href="/post/fender-pink-paisley-telecaster-guide/">1968 and 1969 Paisley Telecaster</a>, and one on <a class="spoke" href="/post/fender-custom-color-authentication-guide/">authenticating a custom color finish</a>, which is where the largest premiums and the largest mistakes both live.</p>
        <p class="note-sm">This guide is one of the model pages behind the <a class="spoke" href={HUB_HREF}>full Fender serial number guide</a>, where the complete year-by-year serial charts live.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="pt0">
      <div class="measure stack">
        <span class="eyebrow">Expert Questions</span>
        <h2>Common Questions About Dating A Telecaster</h2>
        <div class="faqs">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="faq">
              <summary>{f.q}</summary>
              <div class="faqbody"><p>{f.a}</p></div>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- APPRAISAL -->
    <section>
      <div class="measure">
        <div class="appraise" id="appraisal">
          <span class="eyebrow">Free Expert Appraisal</span>
          <h2>Think You Have A Blackguard?</h2>
          <p>The early ones are worth checking properly, because the gap between an original and a good-looking parts guitar runs into serious money. Send Joe three clear photos, the front, the back, and the bridge, for a free expert appraisal within 24 hours. No obligation, and no screwdriver required.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="call" href={hrefs.tel}>Call Or Text Joe: {contact.phone}</a>
            <a class="send" href="/free-appraisal/">Or Send Photos Online</a>
          </div>
          <p class="note-sm">Once you have the year, see what it is worth in the <a class="spoke" href="/vintage-fender-telecaster-value-guide/">Telecaster value guide</a>, or read how to <a class="spoke" href="/sell-my-fender-guitar/">sell your Fender</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BIO -->
    <section class="pt0">
      <div class="measure"><div class="bio" id="about-joe">
        <LicensedImage src={meta("about-me-joe-1.jpg")} alt="Joe Dampt, owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars, holding a vintage guitar" width={150} />
        <div>
          <div class="who"><a href="/about-me/">Joe Dampt</a></div>
          <div class="cred">Owner, Joe's Vintage Guitars · Mesa, AZ · 12 years full-time · 10,000+ appraised · 2,100+ positive reviews</div>
          <p class="biotext">Joe Dampt is the owner of Joe's Vintage Guitars in Mesa, Arizona, where he's spent more than twelve years buying, appraising, and authenticating vintage instruments full-time. He's personally valued over 10,000 guitars for sellers in all 50 states, served as a traveling appraiser for the Treasure Seekers Roadshow, and sourced instruments for Grammy Award-winning artists. Every check on this page comes from that bench work.</p>
        </div>
      </div></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <!-- Floating TOC (sidebar + mobile FAB + sheet), matches the hub pattern -->
  <aside class="fspot-toc" id="fspot-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
    <p class="fspot-toc__label">On This Page</p>
    <ul class="fspot-toc__list">
      {tocItems.map(([href, label]) => (<li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>))}
    </ul>
  </aside>
  <button class="fspot-toc-fab" id="fspot-toc-fab" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="fspot-toc-sheet"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h16"></path></svg>Jump To A Section</button>
  <div class="fspot-toc-sheet" id="fspot-toc-sheet" role="dialog" aria-label="Jump to a section" aria-modal="false">
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__head"><span>Jump To A Section</span><button class="fspot-toc-sheet__close" id="fspot-toc-sheet-close" type="button" aria-label="Close section list">&times;</button></div>
    <div class="fspot-toc-sheet__body" id="fspot-toc-sheet-body"></div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .fspot-header-backdrop{position:relative;isolation:isolate;z-index:0;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__dark{background:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);height:54px;}
  .fspot-header-backdrop__cream{background:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);height:56px;border-bottom:6px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);}

  .fspot{
    --paper:#f5efe3; --panel:#ffffff; --panel-2:#f3ead6; --inset:#fffdf8;
    --ink:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14); --ink-strong:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);
    --muted:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#7a6a54); --line:#ddd0b6;
    --gold:var(--color-brand-gold,#c8983a); --gold-deep:#8c6a24;
    --rust:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e); --rust-bright:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);
    --amber:#f3e3c2; --amber-line:#e3c88a; --darkbrown:#3a2a18; --darkbrown-2:#4a3520;
    --shadow:0 1px 2px rgba(58,40,23,.06), 0 10px 30px rgba(58,40,23,.08);
    --fd:var(--font-display), "Oswald", system-ui, sans-serif;
    --fm:ui-monospace,"SF Mono",Consolas,"Liberation Mono",monospace;
    --measure:min(92vw,820px);
    background:var(--paper); color:var(--ink); font-size:17px; line-height:1.6;
    padding-bottom:1rem;
  }
  .fspot [id]{scroll-margin-top:90px;}
  .fspot h1,.fspot h2,.fspot h3{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;line-height:1.12;margin:0;color:var(--ink-strong);text-wrap:balance;}
  .fspot h1{font-size:clamp(2rem,4.6vw,2.9rem);letter-spacing:.005em;text-transform:uppercase;}
  .fspot h2{font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3.1vw,2rem);letter-spacing:.01em;}
  .fspot h3{font-size:clamp(1.21rem,2.1vw,1.34rem);letter-spacing:.015em;margin-top:.8rem;color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot p{margin:0;}
  .fspot a{color:var(--gold-deep);text-underline-offset:2px;}
  .fspot a:hover{color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot .measure{width:var(--measure);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot section{padding-block:clamp(1.7rem,4vw,2.6rem);}
  .fspot .pt0{padding-top:0;}
  .fspot .rule{height:1px;background:var(--line);width:min(94vw,1080px);margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot .stack{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .note-sm{font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .eyebrow{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.6em;}
  .fspot .eyebrow::before{content:"";width:24px;height:3px;background:var(--gold);}
  .fspot a.spoke{color:var(--gold-deep);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1.5px dotted var(--gold);font-weight:600;}
  .fspot a.spoke:hover{color:var(--rust);border-bottom-color:var(--rust);}
  .fspot a.back{position:relative;z-index:31;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:.78rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.4rem;}
  .fspot a.back::before{content:"\2190";font-size:1rem;}
  .fspot a.back:hover{color:var(--rust-bright);}

  .fspot .hero{padding-top:clamp(1.6rem,4vw,2.6rem);padding-bottom:1.4rem;}
  .fspot .lead{font-size:1.16rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .trust{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem .9rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.76rem;color:var(--muted);}
  .fspot .trust span{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;}
  .fspot .trust span::before{content:"";width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--rust);}

  .fspot .secintro{font-size:.97rem;max-width:70ch;color:var(--ink);}
  .fspot .secintro b{color:var(--ink-strong);}

  .fspot .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;min-width:520px;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .tablewrap.compact table{min-width:0;}
  .fspot th,.fspot td{text-align:left;padding:.6rem 1rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top;font-size:.94rem;}
  .fspot thead th{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.65rem;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot tbody tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0;}
  .fspot tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--panel-2) 45%,transparent);}
  .fspot .code{font-family:var(--fm);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--ink);white-space:nowrap;}
  .fspot .yr{font-family:var(--fm);color:var(--rust);font-weight:600;}

  .fspot .figrow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:1rem;}
  .fspot .figrow.single{grid-template-columns:minmax(230px,540px);}
  .fspot .figrow.gallery{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.4rem;max-width:680px;margin-inline:auto;}
  .fspot figure.sfig{margin:0;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--panel);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot figure.sfig img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;background:var(--panel-2);}
  .fspot figure.sfig figcaption{padding:.55rem .8rem;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted);border-top:1px solid var(--line);}
  .fspot .herofig{margin-top:.3rem;}
  .fspot .herofig figcaption{font-size:.82rem;color:var(--ink);}

  .fspot .tip{background:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--darkbrown-2),var(--darkbrown));color:#f0e1c1;border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.15rem;font-size:.95rem;}
  .fspot .tip::before{content:"JOE'S TIP";display:block;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--gold);font-size:.78rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .tip b{color:#e0be79;}
  .fspot .warnbox{background:#fbe9e6;border:1px solid #e4a99d;border-left:6px solid var(--rust);border-radius:10px;padding:1rem 1.2rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .warnbox__head{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.45rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--rust);font-size:.85rem;margin-bottom:.4rem;}
  .fspot .warnbox__head svg{flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot .warnbox p{margin:0;color:var(--ink);font-size:.97rem;}

  .fspot .faqs{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;}
  .fspot details.faq{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);overflow:hidden;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot details.faq>summary{cursor:pointer;list-style:none;padding:.85rem 1.05rem;font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1rem;color:var(--ink-strong);display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:.8rem;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none;}
  .fspot details.faq>summary::after{content:"+";font-family:var(--fm);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--rust);flex:0 0 auto;}
  .fspot details.faq[open]>summary::after{content:"\2212";}
  .fspot details.faq .faqbody{padding:0 1.05rem 1rem;font-size:.96rem;color:var(--muted);}

  .fspot .appraise{background:var(--amber);border:1px solid var(--amber-line);border-left:5px solid var(--rust);border-radius:12px;padding:clamp(1.4rem,3.5vw,2rem);box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
  .fspot .appraise h2{margin-bottom:.5rem;}
  .fspot .appraise p{color:var(--ink);font-size:1.02rem;}
  .fspot .appraise .row{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.7rem;margin-top:1.1rem;align-items:center;}
  .fspot .appraise .call{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:700;font-size:1.15rem;letter-spacing:.02em;color:#fff;background:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;padding:.7rem 1.3rem;border-radius:8px;}
  .fspot .appraise .call:hover{background:var(--rust-bright);}
  .fspot .appraise .send{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:.86rem;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--rust);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}

  .fspot .bio{display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:center;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;background:var(--panel);}
  .fspot .bio img{flex:0 0 auto;width:76px;height:76px;border-radius:50%;object-fit:cover;border:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .who{font-family:var(--fd);font-weight:600;font-size:1.2rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.02em;}
  .fspot .bio .who a{color:var(--ink-strong);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:2px solid var(--gold);}
  .fspot .bio .cred{font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.2rem;}
  .fspot .bio .biotext{margin-top:.55rem;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--muted);}

  /* mobile card tables */
  @media (max-width:719px){
    .fspot .tablewrap{overflow:visible;border:0;border-radius:0;box-shadow:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap table{min-width:0;width:100%;display:block;background:transparent;}
    .fspot .tablewrap thead{display:none;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:.65rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap tr{display:block;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);padding:.7rem .85rem;box-shadow:var(--shadow);}
    .fspot .tablewrap tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:var(--panel);}
    .fspot .tablewrap td{display:block;border:0;padding:.25rem 0;font-size:.95rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap td::before{content:attr(data-label);display:block;font-family:var(--fm);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.1rem;}
    .fspot .tablewrap .code,.fspot .tablewrap .yr{white-space:normal;}
  }

  /* floating TOC */
  .fspot-toc{display:none;z-index:25;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--color-brand-brown-tan,#d2b48c);border-radius:10px;width:260px;max-height:calc(100vh - 160px);padding:16px 18px;position:fixed;top:120px;right:24px;overflow-y:auto;box-shadow:0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.08);}
  @media (min-width:1440px){.fspot-toc{display:block;}}
  .fspot-toc__label{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.13em;color:var(--color-brand-brown,#3e2a14);border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e2e;margin:0 0 10px;padding-bottom:8px;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;}
  .fspot-toc__list{margin:0;padding:0;font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.4;list-style:none;}
  .fspot-toc__list li{margin:0;}
  .fspot-toc__list a{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);border-radius:4px;padding:5px 6px;text-decoration:none;display:block;}
  .fspot-toc__list a:hover{background:var(--color-brand-cream-warm,#f5ead4);color:var(--color-brand-rust,#a03a1e);}
  .fspot-toc-fab{display:none;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet{display:none;}
  @media (max-width:1439.98px){
    .fspot-toc-fab{z-index:45;background:var(--color-brand-rust-bright,#be4b25);color:#fff;font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;cursor:pointer;border:none;border-radius:999px;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:11px 18px;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;display:inline-flex;position:fixed;bottom:1.5rem;left:16px;box-shadow:0 4px 16px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-fab svg{width:18px;height:18px;}
    .fspot-toc-fab[aria-expanded="true"]{background:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet{z-index:46;opacity:0;visibility:hidden;background:#fff;border:1px solid #a03a1e33;border-radius:12px;flex-direction:column;width:min(340px,100vw - 32px);max-height:60vh;transition:opacity .2s,transform .2s,visibility .2s;display:flex;position:fixed;bottom:5rem;left:16px;overflow-y:auto;transform:translateY(12px);box-shadow:0 10px 34px rgba(0,0,0,.28);}
    .fspot-toc-sheet.fspot-toc-sheet--open{opacity:1;visibility:visible;transform:translateY(0);}
  }
  .fspot-toc-sheet__head{font-family:var(--font-display),sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-brand-rust-dark,#682412);background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #a03a1e26;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;padding:12px 16px;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;display:flex;position:sticky;top:0;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__close{color:var(--color-brand-brown-muted,#6c5238);cursor:pointer;background:none;border:none;padding:0 4px;font-size:24px;line-height:1;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body{padding:8px 10px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list{font-size:14px;}
  .fspot-toc-sheet__body .fspot-toc__list a{padding:8px 6px;}
</style>

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Floating TOC: clone list into the mobile sheet, wire the FAB
    var list = document.querySelector('#fspot-toc .fspot-toc__list');
    var body = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-body');
    if (list && body) body.appendChild(list.cloneNode(true));
    var fab = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-fab'),
        sheet = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet'),
        closeBtn = document.getElementById('fspot-toc-sheet-close');
    function setOpen(o) { if (!sheet) return; sheet.classList.toggle('fspot-toc-sheet--open', o); if (fab) fab.setAttribute('aria-expanded', o ? 'true' : 'false'); }
    if (fab) fab.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!sheet.classList.contains('fspot-toc-sheet--open')); });
    if (closeBtn) closeBtn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (sheet && fab && !sheet.contains(e.target) && !fab.contains(e.target)) setOpen(false); });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
    if (sheet) sheet.addEventListener('click', function (e) { if (e.target.closest('a')) setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
